  "content": {
    "id": "p41-q12",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Integration by substitution is the formal setting out of reverse chain rule. Consider a composite function $f(g(x))$. Show that:\n$$\\int f(g(x))g'(x)\\,dx=\\int f(u)\\,du\\qquad\\text{using }u=g(x).$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle\\int f(g(x))g'(x)\\,dx=\\int f(u)\\,du$.",
      "worked": "Using $u=g(x)$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{du}{dx}&=g'(x),\\\\dx&=\\frac{du}{g'(x)}.\\end{aligned}$$\nHence\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int f(g(x))g'(x)\\,dx&=\\int f(u)g'(x)\\frac{du}{g'(x)}\\\\&=\\int f(u)\\,du.\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 31
  },
